      "text": "To find the value of \\( b + m \\), we start by using the fact that the lines intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\). This means that when \\( x = 6 \\), the \\( y \\)-coordinates of both lines are equal to 10. We can substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equations of the lines.\n\nFirst, substitute into the equation \\( y = mx + 4 \\):\n\\[ 10 = m(6) + 4. \\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[ 10 = 6m + 4. \\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[ 6 = 6m. \\]\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\\[ m = 1. \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation \\( y = 3x + b \\):\n\\[ 10 = 3(6) + b. \\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[ 10 = 18 + b. \\]\nSubtract 18 from both sides:\n\\[ -8 = b. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of \\( b + m \\):\n\\[ b + m = -8 + 1 = -7. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( b + m \\) is \\(\\boxed{-7}\\).",
